WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.217
- You know my next guest
from "One Tree Hill,"

00:00:02.217 --> 00:00:04.420
"Grey's Anatomy," and "White Collar."

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:07.010
This summer she hosted "Friday
Night In With the Morgans"

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:10.340
on AMC with her husband,
Jeffrey Dean Morgan.

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:12.410
Recently she traded
the Hollywood spotlight

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:15.150
for a simpler life on a
farm in upstate New York.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.150
She's written about that

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:18.090
in her new book, "The Rural Diaries."

00:00:18.090 --> 00:00:19.220
It's out right now.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.200
Let's bring her in here.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.010
Hilarie Burton Morgan, how you doing?

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:23.090
- How are you?

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:23.923
- I am so good.

00:00:23.923 --> 00:00:26.057
Oh my gosh, it's so
beautiful where you are!

00:00:26.057 --> 00:00:27.550
- I'm like trying to find a place

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:30.110
where there's not like kids or a mailman

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.560
or like destruction anywhere.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:34.340
It's just, you know how it is.

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:35.860
- That was literally me in Montana.

00:00:35.860 --> 00:00:36.750
Now I'm back in LA,

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:38.970
but every time I had to
do my show from Montana

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:41.330
I was like how do I make this look,

00:00:41.330 --> 00:00:43.730
I was like there's so much
going on here right now.

00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.900
- I think there's like a
lawn mower running somewhere.

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:50.570
- There was always a tractor
or some loud thing happening.

00:00:50.570 --> 00:00:52.460
Well I was just talking
to Al Roker, actually.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:53.790
I was literally just talking to him,

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:55.470
and y'all are in the same
neck of the woods, right?

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.303
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:56.303 --> 00:00:57.970
I think he's up here in the Hudson Valley.

00:00:57.970 --> 00:01:00.003
We, oh look. There's my husband now.

00:01:02.245 --> 00:01:03.960
- I like it.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.010
- They have no idea what we're doing.

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:09.730
Al does a charity with us
called Big Slick in Kansas City,

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:11.130
and so that's the last time we saw him.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:13.540
We raised money for Children's
Mercy Hospital out there,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.290
and we talked about being here.

00:01:15.290 --> 00:01:16.750
The thing about being in
the Hudson Valley, though,

00:01:16.750 --> 00:01:19.070
is that this is where you come to hide out

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:21.359
so you don't ever see anybody.

00:01:21.359 --> 00:01:24.522
- That's why we went to
Montana for our vacation.

00:01:24.522 --> 00:01:25.430
- Yeah.

00:01:25.430 --> 00:01:27.330
- So you're living on a
farm and with your kids.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:28.940
So do you put them to work?

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:31.123
- Oh my god. All the time, yeah.

00:01:31.123 --> 00:01:33.690
- There's so much to get done, right?

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:35.060
- There's so much to get done,

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:37.340
and I want them to be responsible.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:38.580
That was the thing we noticed in LA

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.200
was that a lot of the
kids that we would see

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:42.770
on the playground there weren't aware

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:44.750
that there's other people on the planet.

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:49.360
And so we want our kids to be
grounded and have a lot to do.

00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:51.180
And so we've put 'em to work.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:53.250
Whether it's like building the garden

00:01:53.250 --> 00:01:55.040
or you can hear all the
birds in the background.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:56.023
My son and-

00:01:56.023 --> 00:01:57.330
- I can hear your rooster.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.210
- Dude, my kids are on chicken duty.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:01.043
We just-

00:02:01.043 --> 00:02:03.630
- Yeah, we have chickens,
but I don't allow a rooster.

00:02:03.630 --> 00:02:05.200
I'm like oh gosh.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.480
- We've got such a bad
coyote and fox problem,

00:02:07.480 --> 00:02:10.380
we need a boss.
- Oh, I got you.

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:11.533
- So he's a killer.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:13.790
- That's awesome, though, that
you put your kids to work.

00:02:13.790 --> 00:02:15.970
That's the same thing that
my husband and I were doing.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.450
We wanted our kids to be able to actually,

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.410
because I grew up in the
countryside and so did he.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:22.200
So is it true that you
not only write books,

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.200
shoot a talk show, run a farm,

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.610
but you're also helping
out local hospitals?

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:29.710
- So many of our good friends
here in the Hudson Valley

00:02:29.710 --> 00:02:32.520
are members of the medical field.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.080
Whether it's like doctors
or nurses or EMTs.

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:38.380
And so the immediacy
of helping all of them

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:39.340
was really important.

00:02:39.340 --> 00:02:41.640
So we started sewing masks right away,

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:44.230
encouraging other people to sew masks.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.800
A lot of the moms, it's
like all soccer moms.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.180
We all got together and
started raising money

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.810
to feed all the medical workers

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:53.560
at our COVID testing facility.

00:02:53.560 --> 00:02:55.140
So now that same group of people,

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:57.940
we're taking all that energy
we had for the pandemic,

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.250
and we're putting it towards
our schoolteachers now.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.790
Because we have no idea

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.810
what school is gonna
look like in the fall,

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:05.910
and our teachers can't afford crayons

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.480
and paper and books
and stuff for the kids,

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.660
let alone PPE for
everybody to make it safe.

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:12.934
So, we're trying to-

00:03:12.934 --> 00:03:15.904
- Even thinking about like
a lot of us with kids,

00:03:15.904 --> 00:03:18.270
our kids, they might still be home.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.210
- Yeah.
- And a lot of parents

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.210
don't have access to all those supplies.

00:03:22.357 --> 00:03:25.033
- Not every kid has a computer or an iPad.

00:03:26.200 --> 00:03:28.680
- But your new book is
about your life up there,

00:03:28.680 --> 00:03:29.880
and I think that it's really cool.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:31.667
So what inspired you to do it?

00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:34.430
Was it because basically
you had that other lifestyle

00:03:34.430 --> 00:03:35.940
and you wanted it slowed down a bit?

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:38.100
- Yeah, I mean you know.

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:40.650
We grew up, and it was
like, "You're on TV!"

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.080
And you have to grow up in
front of a lot of people,

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:45.340
and you've gotta project
this certain image.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:48.390
And so when I kind of took
off working to be a mom,

00:03:48.390 --> 00:03:49.670
I didn't do social media.

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.920
I didn't engage in any of that.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.500
And I was encouraged to join social media,

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.070
and what I saw was just kinda unattainable

00:03:58.070 --> 00:03:59.840
and super glamorous.

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:02.140
And it made me feel bad.

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:05.340
I didn't like when I felt
like I was missing out

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:08.240
or that I wasn't living
up to some expectation.

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:10.920
So I started posting just
really like stripped down,

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.600
here's the farm kinda posts,

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.180
and people responded to it.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:16.930
And so I wanted to write a love letter

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:19.170
to our community and our lifestyle

00:04:19.170 --> 00:04:23.000
that glamorized the small,
beautiful, noble lives

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:25.023
that take place when nobody's looking.

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:27.960
Not every has to be on a yacht.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:28.900
You know what I mean?

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.690
- Yeah, well what kind of
animals do you have on the farm?

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:35.440
- Well I've got, Jeffrey has
corralled our new baby chicken.

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:36.273
Can you say hi?

00:04:36.273 --> 00:04:37.590
- Oh, hi! What's up?

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:38.423
- Hey, how are ya?

00:04:38.423 --> 00:04:39.256
Nice to see ya.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:42.780
- Oh my gosh! So cute!

00:04:42.780 --> 00:04:45.940
And I know you have chickens
and ducks like that.

00:04:45.940 --> 00:04:47.360
But what else do you have?

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:49.200
- This is the donkey whisperer.

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:51.750
He spends all day brushing
the donkeys in the field.

00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.510
Here, do you wanna sit down?

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:54.870
Cuddle up, Dad.

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:55.980
We've got alpaca.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:04:59.290
We've got a lot of mini
donkeys we've rescued,

00:04:59.290 --> 00:05:00.530
Jack and Diane.

00:05:00.530 --> 00:05:04.860
- Highland cattle, and then
every spring we take in cows

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:07.720
from the local dairy farm that we raise,

00:05:07.720 --> 00:05:09.643
yearlings with their moms.

00:05:09.643 --> 00:05:12.590
- Yeah, so a cow/calf operation.

00:05:12.590 --> 00:05:13.900
Okay, cool. That's cool.

00:05:13.900 --> 00:05:16.020
- We get calls all the time.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:18.380
People are like, "Oh,
this farm is closing down.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:19.650
Will you take our animals."

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.700
So even if we can't take them,

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:24.560
we try to hook them up
with other people who can.

00:05:24.560 --> 00:05:25.590
- No, that's so hilarious.

00:05:25.590 --> 00:05:26.610
That happened to me in Texas.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:27.443
I had a rescue ranch,

00:05:27.443 --> 00:05:30.240
and like literally people were
just dropping animals out.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.650
And I was like, "Okay, there comes a point

00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.526
to where we won't be able
to get to all of them."

00:05:34.526 --> 00:05:35.960
So that does happen.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:37.010
- It's just us.

00:05:37.010 --> 00:05:37.843
- Yeah.

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:40.780
It was me and my sister and her family,

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:43.210
and I was like, "People don't work here."

00:05:43.210 --> 00:05:44.606
- No!

00:05:44.606 --> 00:05:45.816
- It's just us holding it down.

00:05:45.816 --> 00:05:50.650
So I would just wanna say, everybody,

00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.150
obviously Jeffrey Dean Morgan,

00:05:53.673 --> 00:05:55.703
he's now involved in the
interview. And I'm such a big fan.

00:05:55.703 --> 00:05:56.536
I love "The Walking Dead"

00:05:56.536 --> 00:05:57.773
so I'm just gonna say that shout out.

00:05:57.773 --> 00:05:58.981
- Thank you.

00:05:58.981 --> 00:06:00.382
(voice breaks up)

00:06:00.382 --> 00:06:02.670
- Thank you for hanging out with us.

00:06:02.670 --> 00:06:05.580
So Hilarie told us how special
your community is up there.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:07.000
What's your favorite part about it?

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:09.130
She told us why she kinda
liked living up there

00:06:09.130 --> 00:06:10.390
and out of the limelight.

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:11.300
I love it as well, obviously.

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:13.310
We have a place in Montana.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:15.700
But I wanna know why you
like that kind of life

00:06:15.700 --> 00:06:17.840
but also I heard you owned a candy shop.

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:19.790
Do you still own a candy shop?

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:20.958
- We do.

00:06:20.958 --> 00:06:23.503
I think just getting out
of Los Angeles for us,

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:28.720
it was so needed, I think,
just for our well-being.

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.710
And then having kids, and it started out

00:06:31.710 --> 00:06:33.980
with obviously just the one, Gus.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:36.830
And we thought that this kind of lifestyle

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:40.563
was very conducive to raising a good kid.

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.042
And so far, so good.

00:06:43.042 --> 00:06:44.268
- He's good for now!

00:06:44.268 --> 00:06:47.276
- He's good. He's alive, he's breathing.

00:06:47.276 --> 00:06:48.380
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:49.213
He's pretty good.

00:06:49.213 --> 00:06:53.840
And George is a real animal
whisperer too, it turns out.

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:55.833
Her and I are the, we love our animals.

00:06:55.833 --> 00:06:57.570
- Gus and I are the gardeners.

00:06:57.570 --> 00:06:58.403
They're the animal people.

00:06:58.403 --> 00:07:01.900
- Yeah, she spends a lot of
time in the chicken coops.

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:03.420
She loves the chickens.

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.030
She loves all the animals,
but she loves the chickens.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.490
Like every night it's a thing.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:10.540
She has to have her chicken and duck time.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:11.637
- That's so cool, too,
to have both worlds.

00:07:11.637 --> 00:07:14.310
- Thank you guys so much
for hanging out with us!

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:15.480
I love your beautiful farm!

00:07:15.480 --> 00:07:16.980
- Thank you for having us!

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.147
- Y'all be sure to pick
up Hilarie's new book,

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:20.570
"The Rural Diaries."

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:22.283
Rural is hard for me to say.

